DurableStorage	native	topic	
AtMostOnce	unsupported	none	
AtLeastOnce	native	topic	
ExactlyOnceDelivery	unsupported	none	
ExplicitAck	native	topic	
Nack	native	topic	
DelayedRedelivery	conditional	topic	NackOptions.Delay travels as the `modified` outcome with the message annotation x-opt-redelivery-delay merged in, up to 1h0m0s
ScheduledPublish	conditional	topic	PublishOptions.Delay and DeliverAt travel as the message annotation x-opt-delivery-delay, up to 1h0m0s
MessageTTL	native	topic	PublishOptions.TTL travels as the AMQP message header's `ttl` in milliseconds; what the node does when it expires is the broker's policy
Priority	native	topic	PublishOptions.Priority travels as the AMQP message header's `priority`; a broker that does not order by priority accepts the field and ignores it, which is why a profile that knows so withdraws this claim
DeadLetter	native	topic	
ConsumerGroups	native	group	
DurableSubscriptions	native	group	
FanOut	native	topic	
Replay	unsupported	none	a receiving link attaches to an address and reads what is there; AMQP 1.0 has no position, so there is nothing to rewind
SeekByTime	unsupported	none	
SeekBySequence	unsupported	none	
OrderedByKey	conditional	key	Message.Key travels as the AMQP group-id property and this broker orders a message group
OrderedByPartition	unsupported	none	
OrderedGlobally	unsupported	none	
NativeHeaders	native	topic	
BinaryHeaders	native	topic	a header value that is not valid UTF-8 travels as an AMQP binary application property and comes back byte for byte; a value that is valid UTF-8 travels as a string, so a non-iqmq consumer reads it
DuplicateHeaders	unsupported	none	application-properties is a map, so one key cannot carry two values; MetadataPortableEnvelope and MetadataAdaptive carry a repeated key in the envelope instead
BatchPublish	emulatedvolatile	topic	AMQP 1.0 has no batched transfer: the core publishes every message of the batch and this driver awaits each disposition, so a batch is NOT atomic -- a failure part-way through leaves the messages before it published
AtomicBatchPublish	unsupported	none	
BatchReceive	native	topic	
BatchSettle	unsupported	none	a disposition frame can cover a range of delivery ids and go-amqp exposes only the per-message wrappers, so a batched settlement here would be a loop
ExtendLease	unsupported	none	AMQP 1.0 has no lease: an unsettled delivery is this receiver's for as long as the link lives, so there is no deadline to extend and DeliveryInfo.LeaseExpires is the zero time
IdempotentPublish	conditional	topic	PublishOptions.DedupKey travels as the application property _AMQ_DUPL_ID; it de-duplicates PUBLICATIONS inside whatever window the broker keeps and never de-duplicates deliveries
Deduplication	conditional	topic	PublishOptions.DedupKey travels as the application property _AMQ_DUPL_ID; it de-duplicates PUBLICATIONS inside whatever window the broker keeps and never de-duplicates deliveries
Transactions	unsupported	none	
ServerFilter	conditional	topic	the receiving link's source address is the only filter the portable API can express; an AMQP 1.0 selector filter goes through ConsumerOptions.Filters, which is this driver's own typed configuration
WildcardSubscribe	unsupported	none	
Admin	unsupported	none	
RequestReply	unsupported	none	
PublisherConfirm	native	topic	the peer's `accepted` disposition for the transfer, which ConfirmAccepted waits for; ConfirmNone sends the transfer pre-settled and waits for nothing
DurableConfirm	native	topic	the accepted disposition from a durable queue, which this broker writes before it accepts
NativeTracing	unsupported	none	
Receipts	conditional	topic	the receipt carries the confirmation boundary and the time the disposition arrived; AMQP 1.0's disposition assigns no identifier, so PublishReceipt.MessageID stays empty and Partition, Offset and Sequence stay zero
limit	MaxMessageBytes	1048576
limit	MaxBatchMessages	unknown
limit	MaxBatchBytes	unknown
limit	MaxHeaderCount	unknown
limit	MaxHeaderBytes	unknown
limit	MaxDelay	3600000000000
limit	MaxLease	unknown
